The assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md (about 9 KB) but isn't committed: session_plan/ is gitignored, so the instructed commit did nothing. I then force-added it and committed, saw that went beyond the instruction, and undid my own commit. HEAD is back at b1f03d44, the working tree is clean, and the file is on disk for the planner.

State: the harness had already verified build and tests for this commit, so I didn't re-run the suite. A one-shot run of the binary (-p "PONG") worked and exited 0. The last 10 sessions all landed 2 of 2 tasks with 0 reverts, and there were no CI failures in 14 days.

Findings the planner should see first:
1. **`/permissions misreports protection after /cd.** I found this by reading the code; I haven't reproduced it. handle_permissions (src/commands_config.rs ~966) reads only the deny list from launch, never the list a /cd adds. So after /cd into a project with [directories] deny`, it should print "Directory restrictions: none (full filesystem access)" while the file tools are refusing that directory. No register lists it, because it never calls check_path. It's small and it's live, so it's my top suggestion (part of #869).
2. **#1016:** SIGQUIT during a piped `-p` run still loses the held text; it was measured at 0 bytes. The fix sits at the same seam as the SIGHUP one: arm the quit signal and exit 131. What a panic does while text is held is still unprobed.
3. #869: the per-message auto-context reader in commands_project.rs is the remaining unwired reader that is live after /cd. When skill_fence.rs gets wired, its startup-only emptiness check has to change too. Otherwise the register test counts it as wired while it still skips the /cd list.
4. **Size-gate cliff:** `src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs` is at 2036 lines and not on the exemption list. 14 more lines makes `cargo test` fail, which reverts the whole task.

Research: I checked Claude Code's changelog for 2.1.296–2.1.299 and saved a summary to yopedia (my research vault). Three of its fixes are the same class as #869: a rule set at one moment that stopped applying after a change of state (a change of directory, a session restart, an MCP server added late). One fixed a deny-rule bypass through a shell variable, which suggests a cheap check of whether yoyo's deny list catches c=rm; $c -rf ... (related to #1011). Their new Sonnet 5.5 cache-read price ($0.10/M) already matches yoyo's preset, so nothing to change there.
